Hello,
I am reporting an iSCSI/LIO task-ordering failure where a younger SIMPLE RE=
AD(16) completed and returned stale data while two older WRITE(16) commands=
, including an ORDERED WRITE(16), were still waiting for Data-Out.
The short version is:

  *   one iSCSI Normal session, one I_T nexus, one LUN, one task set;
  *
command sequence: SIMPLE WRITE(16), ORDERED WRITE(16), younger SIMPLE READ(=
16), all for the same LBA range;
  *   the target issued R2Ts for both writes;
  *
before the target received the writes=92 remaining Data-Out and before eith=
er write completed, it completed the younger read with the old data;
  *
after both writes completed GOOD, a follow-up read returned the ORDERED wri=
te data.

That appears to violate the ORDERED task attribute semantics: the ORDERED t=
ask should not be enabled before all older tasks in the task set complete, =
and the younger SIMPLE read should not be enabled before the ORDERED task c=
ompletes.
Kernel and target environment
Target host:

Linux target.local.camicom.com 5.15.0-3.60.5.1.el9uek.x86_64 #2 SMP Wed Oct=
 19 20:27:31 PDT 2022 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux
kernel-uek-5.15.0-3.60.5.1.el9uek.x86_64
targetcli-2.1.57-3.0.1.el9.noarch
python3-rtslib-2.1.76-1.0.1.el9.noarch
target-restore-2.1.76-1.0.1.el9.noarch


Relevant LIO configuration:

target IQN: iqn.2026-07.com.camicom:no-zero-lun-300
TPG: tpg1, no-gen-acls, no-auth
portals: 172.16.7.1:3260 and 172.17.7.1:3260
backstore: fileio/no_zero_lun_300_lun1
path: /var/lib/target/backstores/no-zero-lun-300-lun1.img
size: 4.0 GiB
ALUA state: Active/optimized
tested ACL: iqn.2014-07.com.vmssoftware:test-1 mapped to LUN 1 rw


Initiator host:

Linux init.local.camicom.com 5.15.0-3.60.5.1.el9uek.x86_64 #2 SMP Wed Oct 1=
9 20:27:31 PDT 2022 x86_64x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux
initiator source address: 172.16.7.2
target portal used by reproducer: 172.16.7.1:3260
route: 172.16.7.1 from 172.16.7.2 dev ens3


Target kernel log for the reproducer window had no entries. Initiator kerne=
l log for the same window only showed tcpdump entering and leaving promiscu=
ous mode.
Standalone reproducer
I reproduced this with a standalone C program built against an unmodified f=
resh upstream libiscsi tree:

libiscsi repo: https://github.com/sahlberg/libiscsi.git
libiscsi commit: ef5491376fbae41a0512f06ae2471909523d49d9


libiscsi was configured as:

./autogen.sh
./configure --disable-werror --disable-test-tool --disable-tests \
--disable-examples --disable-manpages --without-gnutls --without-libgcrypt
make -j$(nproc)


The reproducer was built from lio_ordered_write_pending_repro.c with the at=
tached build-with-upstream-libiscsi.sh script.
Run command:
env -u LIBISCSI_CHAP_USERNAME -u LIBISCSI_CHAP_PASSWORD -u LIBISCSI_CHAP_TA=
RGET_USERNAME -u LIBISCSI_CHAP_TARGET_PASSWORD ./reproducer/lio_ordered_wri=
te_pending_repro --destructive --initiator iqn.2014-07.com.vmssoftware:test=
-1 iscsi://172.16.7.1:3260/iqn.2026-07.com.camicom:no-zero-lun-300/1
The reproducer negotiates InitialR2T=3DYes and ImmediateData=3DNo, saves th=
e original 1 MiB scratch extent, initializes it to 0x31, dispatches these t=
hree SCSI Command PDUs before servicing inbound responses, then restores an=
d verifies the scratch extent:

  1.  SIMPLE WRITE(16), pattern 0x52
  2.  ORDERED WRITE(16), pattern 0x73
  3.  SIMPLE READ(16)

The reproducer returned exit status 1 because it observed the bug, and prin=
ted:

SIMPLE WRITE(16): ITT=3D0x2a906ccf CmdSN=3D1575330971 status=3DGOOD
ORDERED WRITE(16): ITT=3D0x2a906cd0 CmdSN=3D1575330972 status=3DGOOD
SIMPLE READ(16): ITT=3D0x2a906cd1 CmdSN=3D1575330973 status=3DGOOD
BUG: younger SIMPLE read returned old 0x31 data while final data is ORDERED=
 0x73 data
scratch restored and verified


Packet evidence
The primary capture is attached as
 reproducer-primary.pcap.gz
tcpdump reported 422 packets captured, 433 received by filter, and 0 packet=
s dropped by kernel.
Derived summary of the relevant iSCSI PDUs:

frame 195: SIMPLE WRITE(16), ITT 0x2a906ccf, CmdSN 1575330971, LBA 0, block=
s 2048
frame 196: ORDERED WRITE(16), ITT 0x2a906cd0, CmdSN 1575330972, LBA 0, bloc=
ks 2048
frame 196: SIMPLE READ(16), ITT 0x2a906cd1, CmdSN 1575330973, LBA 0, blocks=
 2048
frames 197-198: target R2Ts for both writes
frames 208-236: target returns the younger read, all data classified as 0x3=
1, final Data-In has GOOD status
frames 246-296: target requests and receives more Data-Out for both older w=
rites after the read completed
frame 306: older SIMPLE WRITE completes GOOD
frame 308: older ORDERED WRITE completes GOOD
frames 312-339: follow-up read returns ORDERED 0x73 data and completes GOOD


The derived TSV/JSON summaries include frame numbers, ITTs, CmdSNs, CDB opc=
odes, LBA/block counts, DataSNs, buffer offsets, status, and payload classi=
fication by constant byte and SHA-256. They intentionally do not dump raw p=
ayload bytes.
Current upstream source path
I also checked current upstream Linux source. The exact snapshot used for t=
his source review was torvalds/linux master:

fce2dfa773ced15f27dd27cd0b482a7473cdcf2a


Relevant links:

  *   https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/fce2dfa773ced15f27dd27cd0b482a=
7473cdcf2a/drivers/target/target_core_transport.c#L2257-L2343
  *   https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/fce2dfa773ced15f27dd27cd0b482a=
7473cdcf2a/drivers/target/target_core_transport.c#L2824-L2922
  *   https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/fce2dfa773ced15f27dd27cd0b482a=
7473cdcf2a/drivers/target/iscsi/iscsi_target_erl1.c#L916-L950

My reading of that path is:

  *
target-core applies task-attribute scheduling in target_execute_cmd() via t=
arget_handle_task_attr();
  *
for writes whose data is not yet present, transport_generic_new_cmd() puts =
the command in TRANSPORT_WRITE_PENDING and calls the fabric write_pending()=
 hook before target_execute_cmd() has applied the ORDERED barrier;
  *   the iSCSI command path can submit such a write while waiting for Data=
-Out;
  *
a younger READ is not write-pending, so it can proceed to target_execute_cm=
d() and execute before the older ORDERED write has established its barrier.

That source path matches the wire capture: the ORDERED write was still wait=
ing for Data-Out when the younger SIMPLE read completed.
I can retest patches or collect a trace against a different kernel if usefu=
l.


